{title: Come, Thou Fount of Every Blessing}
{artist: Robert Robinson}
{key: D}
{tempo: 84}
{time: 3/4}
{meta: tune NETTLETON - Wyeth's Repository of Sacred Music, Part Second (1813)}
{meta: public_domain Text 1758, tune 1813: both published before 1931 (US public domain); all authors, translators and composers died over 70 years ago. Chords and section layout: original simple arrangement by Tabernacle Tools. Original verses only, no modern additions.}

{start_of_verse: Verse 1}
[D]Come, Thou Fount of [G]every [D]blessing,
Tune my heart to [A]sing Thy [D]grace;
Streams of mercy, [G]never [D]ceasing,
Call for songs of [A]loudest [D]praise.
[D]Teach me some me[G]lodious [D]sonnet,
Sung by flaming [A]tongues a[D]bove;
Praise the mount! I'm [G]fixed up[D]on it,
Mount of Thy re[A]deeming [D]love.
{end_of_verse}
{start_of_verse: Verse 2}
[D]Here I raise mine [G]Ebe[D]nezer;
Hither by Thy [A]help I'm [D]come;
And I hope, by [G]Thy good [D]pleasure,
Safely to ar[A]rive at [D]home.
[D]Jesus sought me [G]when a [D]stranger,
Wandering from the [A]fold of [D]God;
He, to rescue [G]me from [D]danger,
Interposed His [A]precious [D]blood.
{end_of_verse}
{start_of_verse: Verse 3}
[D]O to grace how [G]great a [D]debtor
Daily I'm con[A]strained to [D]be!
Let Thy goodness, [G]like a [D]fetter,
Bind my wandering [A]heart to [D]Thee.
[D]Prone to wander, [G]Lord, I [D]feel it,
Prone to leave the [A]God I [D]love;
Here's my heart, O [G]take and [D]seal it,
Seal it for Thy [A]courts a[D]bove.
{end_of_verse}
